WRONG: SAT asks you to throw your intuitions about reality out the window. RIGHT: SAT asks you to come back to your senses, to recognize the simple geometric logic of pictures on paper; Minkowski and Einstein had the tool—the worldline diagram—that allows that intuition to work, and to translate it down from four dimensions to three, to bring that intuition into focus, and to notice how well our basic intuitions allow us to draw productive conclusions about the universe we find ourselves in in a way that solves problems.

﻿

WRONG: A theory can be proven right. RIGHT: A theory can only be proven wrong; any theory can be tinkered to work when that happens, and that tinkering produces—depending on how you look at it, and how you tinker—either a whole new theory or a new refinement of the theory.

﻿

WRONG: The Z3 fusion gate means Q=4 particles cannot exist. RIGHT: It *may* mean that, but I suspect this has the logic backwards; Q=1, 2, or 3 are easy to form naturally, but reality doesn't “break” for tetraquarks, Q=4 is just so energetically disfavored that it almost requires engineering to create such a braid—further, if one were to engineer it, then it would probably be hyper-stabile. The right framing is usually this: What would rope do? If you can shake a rope into a 4-strand braid, then we expect tetraquarks to exist in nature, if not, not.

﻿

WRONG: Q=4 means “a four-strand braid”. RIGHT: Q numbering proper is the number of *tightest orderly braiding*, that is basically the same as lowest energy state; a hadron braids filaments as tight as possible; but a hydrogen atom is—loosely speaking—a Q=4 braid, it’s just that one of those filaments (the electron) is loosely wrapped around the tight hadron braid. In fact, we should probably clarify this by introducing a separate counting—Q=tightest, L=loose, and each should have a scale factor associated Q_1=tightest quark braid, Q_2=tightest hadronic braid; thus, He-3 becomes Q_1=3 (as with all atoms), Q_2=3 (3 nucleons), o=2 (braid ordering [possibly but not necessarily identical to superhelical coil ordering] includes 1 inner [quarkic] tight braiding of 3 sets, 1 outer [hadronic] tight braid of two protons and a neutron, L=2 (electron shell), Q_tot=3o3 QL_tot=11 (counting electrons). Tightening up these conventions will be necessary for the next iteration of the theory.

﻿

